The word 'misbranded' as defined under Clause (ix) of Section 2 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 reads thus :
"(ix) "misbranded"--an article of food shall be deemed to be misbranded- (a) if it is an imitation of, or is a substitute for, or resembles in a manner likely to deceive, another article of food under the name of which it is sold, and is not plainly and conspicuously labelled so as to indicate its true character;
(b) if it is falsely stated to be the product of any place or country;
(c) if it is sold by a name which belongs to another article of food;
(d) if it is so coloured, flavoured or coated, powdered or polished that the fact that the article is damaged is concealed or if the articles is made to appear better or of greater value than it really is;
